{"name":"Vegan Green","hex":"#006c47","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"vegan","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a product or practice, especially food, not containing animal products (meat, eggs, milk, leather, etc.) or inherently involving animal use.","Of a person, committed to avoiding any product or practice that inherently involves animal use."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who does not eat, drink or otherwise consume any animal products","A person committed to avoiding products and practices that inherently involve animal use, including all foods containing animal products, and to abstaining from direct and intentional harm to animals as far as possible; an adherent to veganism."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/vegan"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Veganism","blocks":[{"text":"Veganism is the practice of abstaining from the use of animal products and the consumption of animal source foods, and an associated philosophy that rejects the commodity status of animals. A person who practices veganism is known as a vegan; the word is also used to describe foods and materials that are compatible with veganism."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/Vegan_friendly_icon.svg/330px-Vegan_friendly_icon.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Veganism","wikidata":"Q181138"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Gandhi_LVS_1931.jpg/500px-Gandhi_LVS_1931.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Gandhi_LVS_1931.jpg/500px-Gandhi_LVS_1931.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mahatma Gandhi, London Vegetarian Society, 20 November 1931, with Henry S. Salt on his right[f]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGandhi_LVS_1931.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Barbara_Moore_%281961%29.jpg/500px-Barbara_Moore_%281961%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Barbara_Moore_%281961%29.jpg/1280px-Barbara_Moore_%281961%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&u